====================================================================================================
RICHARDSON ON V6:  does the T^(-1/3) exponent move?
====================================================================================================
  Both arms get the best R from a grid at every budget, so neither is
  handicapped by a frozen radius. Richardson spends its budget 26/74 across
  the two radii, and costs 2G circuit executions against G.

    N   T total  circuits  1-cos plain  R* plain  1-cos rich  R* rich   gain
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    4      4096     3 / 6      0.25835      0.60     0.30722     0.90   0.84
    4     16384     3 / 6      0.07809      0.60     0.20487     0.90   0.38
    4     65536     3 / 6      0.05174      0.60     0.09918     0.90   0.52
    4    262144     3 / 6      0.01688      0.45     0.03113     0.90   0.54
       fitted 1-cos ~ T^beta:   plain -0.620   richardson -0.548
       predicted:               plain -0.667      richardson -0.800
  ..........................................................................

  1-cos tracks error SQUARED, so the predicted exponents are twice those on
  the error itself: -2/3 for the T^(-1/3) estimator and -4/5 after the bias
  is cancelled. 'gain' is plain error over Richardson error at equal TOTAL
  shots, already charging Richardson its doubled circuit count.
